<!-- Problems -->
<section class="section" id="problems">
  <div class="section-label">01 — Problem Space</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">The current system wasn't designed for humans.</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Dutch parking meters are a masterclass in function-first design that ignores how people actually behave under pressure, in bad weather, in a hurry.</p>

  <div class="problems-grid">
    <div class="problem-card">
      <div class="problem-icon">🧠</div>
      <h3>Cognitive Overload</h3>
      <p>Multiple screens, physical keyboards, card slots, and payment areas force users to context-switch constantly across the interface.</p>
      <div class="problem-stat">5+ distinct interaction zones</div>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-card">
      <div class="problem-icon">⌨️</div>
      <h3>ABC Keyboard Layout</h3>
      <p>Alphabetically ordered buttons defy decades of muscle memory. Small targets make errors near-certain for older users or anyone in gloves.</p>
      <div class="problem-stat">~3x slower than QWERTY</div>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-card">
      <div class="problem-icon">🌧️</div>
      <h3>No Weather Resilience</h3>
      <p>Reflective screens become unreadable in sunlight. Wet fingers fail on physical buttons. No consideration for Dutch weather realities.</p>
      <div class="problem-stat">200+ rainy days/year in NL</div>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-card">
      <div class="problem-icon">🌐</div>
      <h3>Language Barrier</h3>
      <p>Text-heavy instructions in a single language alienate tourists and non-native speakers, creating anxiety about payment success.</p>
      <div class="problem-stat">20M+ tourists/year to NL</div>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-card">
      <div class="problem-icon">♿</div>
      <h3>Inaccessible Design</h3>
      <p>Tiny buttons, no audio feedback, no screen reader support, and fixed-height placement excludes wheelchair users entirely.</p>
      <div class="problem-stat">Zero accessibility features</div>
    </div>
    <div class="problem-card">
      <div class="problem-icon">⏱️</div>
      <h3>45-60 Second Transactions</h3>
      <p>The cumulative friction of bad layout, bad input, and bad feedback turns a simple payment into a full minute of frustration.</p>
      <div class="problem-stat">Up to 60s per transaction</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Design Principles -->
<section class="section" id="solution">
  <div class="section-label">02 — Design Principles</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Three rules. Zero exceptions.</h2>
  <p class="section-desc">Every design decision was filtered through these principles. If it didn't serve at least one, it didn't ship.</p>

  <div class="principles">
    <div class="principle">
      <div class="principle-number">01</div>
      <h3>Familiar by Default</h3>
      <p>Leverage smartphone patterns people already know. Zero learning curve. If your grandmother can use Apple Pay, she can use this.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <div class="principle-number">02</div>
      <h3>Weather-Proof UX</h3>
      <p>Every interaction must work in rain, gloves, and direct sunlight. If it fails in February in Amsterdam, it fails entirely.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="principle">
      <div class="principle-number">03</div>
      <h3>Graceful Degradation</h3>
      <p>QR fails? Manual entry works. NFC fails? Card slot available. No connectivity? Offline mode queues payment. Always a path forward.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

